{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>You and all your assembly, be, etc.</b> It is difficult: it should have said <b>you and all your assembly and Aaron</b>, etc., and then it would not have needed to go back and say <b>you and they</b>. Perhaps it intended to summon those who had gathered to come tomorrow to clarify the matter, and in this it intended that they not slip away from day to day and that lovers of strife multiply upon them — therefore He wished to compel them to come tomorrow before HaShem. Accordingly He summoned none other than those who came in the strife — Korah and his assembly — unlike Aaron, except that He mentioned him [instructing him] to take his firepan with them." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to say that they should prepare themselves in holiness and purity, so that they would be fit to be before HaShem — and that is the statement <b>be before HaShem</b>. And perhaps for this He gave them time and said <b>tomorrow</b>, and in this particular He did not include Aaron with them, for he is prepared and ready — except that He mentioned him afterward and said <b>you and they and Aaron</b>, that he too should be with them. And although He had already told them the very same things above — as it is written ״קְחוּ לָכֶם מַחְתּוֹת וְגוֹ׳ לִפְנֵי ה׳ מָחָר״ — because he rebuked them afterward and said to them ״הַמְעַט מִכֶּם וְגוֹ׳ רַב לָכֶם בְּנֵי לֵוִי״ etc., the substance of those words would reverse what He had said earlier. Therefore, when they did not accept the reproof, He returned to say the first things, to make the test of the incense."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:10:07.763335899Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}